---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
title: "FAQ";
description: "Frequently Asked Questions about Umi‑Time.";
---

<Base title="FAQ: Umi-Time">
  <div class="umi-region umi-pad">
    <div class="faq">
      <h1>Frequently Asked Questions about Umi‑Time</h1>

      <h2>🌍 Conceptual Understanding</h2>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">What is Umi‑Time in one sentence?</button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            At its core, Umi‑Time is UTC expressed as a single 360‑umi daily
            rotation, giving the entire planet one shared continuous time while
            preserving local meaning through presentation.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >Why does the world need another time system?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Civil time was built for local life in a world divided by borders.
            Today’s world is planetary, yet our clocks remain fragmented by time
            zones, seasonal offsets, and political decisions. Umi‑Time offers a
            single, coherent way to represent the day everywhere on Earth — a
            modern alternative for a civilization that now thinks and operates
            globally.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >How is Umi‑Time different from civil time (UTC / local time)?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Civil time, UTC, and Umi‑Time represent the day in fundamentally
            different ways:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Civil time</strong> uses 12‑hour clocks, divides the world into
              time zones, and applies seasonal offsets like daylight saving. It works
              for local schedules but becomes inconsistent and fragmented at global
              scale.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>UTC</strong> is a universal 24‑hour standard with no time zones
              or seasonal adjustments. It is ideal for coordination, but its hours
              have no relationship to the observer's experience of the day.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Umi‑Time</strong> is a universal 360‑umi cycle mapped directly
              from UTC. It has no time zones or seasonal offsets, but unlike UTC,
              it can include optional local context layers, allowing interfaces to
              show how the global daily cycle aligns with your local daylight.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >Is Umi‑Time meant to replace existing time standards?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            It can potentially replace civil time, if society finds greater
            value in it. For now, Umi‑Time coexists with civil time and
            complements it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >Is Umi‑Time a scientific time scale or a cultural one?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Neither. Umi‑Time is a representational layer built on top of UTC.
            Scientific time scales define when events occur with precision;
            cultural time systems define how societies choose to express the
            day. Umi‑Time sits between these worlds — keeping UTC’s stability
            while presenting the daily cycle in a form humans can read, share,
            and reason about.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">Does Umi‑Time depend on time zones?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>No. Umi‑Time has no time zones and does not depend on them.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >Is Umi‑Time the same everywhere on Earth?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Yes. The Umi‑Time value is identical everywhere. Only the optional
            local context layers depend on location.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>🧭 How It Works</h2>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">What exactly is umi in Umi‑Time?</button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Umi is the core unit of Umi‑Time, dividing the UTC day into 360
            equal parts. The umi value represents UTC's progress through its
            daily cycle.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">What does the Umi dial represent?</button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            The dial is a visual map of UTC’s daily progression. The hand shows
            the current Umi‑Time; optional layers can display local solar‑day
            features such as sunrise, sunset, and seasonal shifts.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">How is the umi value calculated?</button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            <code>umi = (UTC seconds of day ÷ 86,400) × 360</code>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >Why does the hand rotate once per day instead of twice?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Because Umi‑Time maps the UTC day onto a single circular rotation,
            making the progression of the day directly visible.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">Why is solar geometry involved?</button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Solar geometry adds optional local meaning — sunrise, sunset,
            twilight, day length, and seasonal variation — without affecting the
            umi value itself.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >Does Umi‑Time drift relative to UTC?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            No. Umi‑Time is a direct mapping of UTC’s 24‑hour day onto 360ᵘ and
            stays perfectly synchronized with it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">How precise is Umi‑Time?</button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Exactly as precise as UTC, since it is derived directly from UTC
            time.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >Does Umi‑Time require astronomical data or can it be computed
          locally?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Umi‑Time itself requires no astronomical data — it is computed
            entirely from UTC. Optional solar‑context layers may use
            astronomical inputs.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>🕰️ Using Umi‑Time</h2>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">How do I read the Umi‑Time clock?</button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            The hand shows your current Umi‑Time on the dial. Its position on
            the solar ring indicates your local solar‑day phase, such as
            sunrise, daylight, twilight, or night.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >How do I convert between Umi‑Time and UTC or local civil time?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            <code>UTC_seconds = (umi ÷ 360ᵘ) × 86,400</code>. Convert the
            resulting UTC time to local civil time using your time zone.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">Can I use Umi‑Time for scheduling?</button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Yes — if everyone involved uses Umi‑Time. Otherwise, convert between
            systems as needed.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >Is there a recommended way to display Umi‑Time on devices?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            A common pattern is the standard Umi‑Time analogclock: a fixed solar
            ring, a dial rotated by longitude, and a single hand that completes
            one rotation per UTC day.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">Does Umi‑Time work offline?</button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Yes. Umi‑Time comes directly from your device’s UTC clock. Solar‑day
            features can also be computed offline so long as the observer's
            location is known.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >Is there an API or library I can use?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Yes. Official libraries compute Umi‑Time, provide solar‑context
            helpers, and ensure consistent behavior across platforms.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >Can I embed the Umi‑Time clock on my own website?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Yes. A lightweight <a href="/examples/clock"
              >Umi‑Time clock web component</a
            > is available for embedding.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>🗺️ Map & Solar Geometry</h2>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >Why does the map demo show a shadow?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            It visualizes the nighttime side of Earth using the solar terminator
            — the boundary between day and night.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >Why does the map show Greenland as large as Africa?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            The Mercator projection exaggerates the size of high‑latitude
            regions, making Greenland appear much larger than it is.
          </p>
          <p>
            The demo uses Mercator intentionally: it keeps latitude and
            longitude lines straight, which makes the solar terminator, seasonal
            shadow, and other Umi‑Time overlays behave predictably on screen.
            The goal of the map is to show how daylight moves across Earth in
            real time — not to depict land‑mass sizes accurately.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >What does the seasonal slider represent?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            It represents the day of the year. Adjusting it changes the Sun’s
            declination, which affects both the map’s day‑night shadow and the
            solar ring on the clock.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >Why does the terminator line look curved?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Because it’s a great circle on a spherical Earth projected onto a
            flat map. The projection makes straight lines appear curved.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >How does latitude affect the Umi dial?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Latitude changes the solar‑day markers — sunrise, sunset, twilight,
            and day length — but it does not affect the umi value or the dial
            itself.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >Does Umi‑Time behave differently near the poles?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>The umi value works the same everywhere, including the poles.</p>
          <p>
            What changes is the solar‑day context: long stretches of daylight or
            darkness, unusual twilight patterns, and periods with no sunrise or
            sunset. These affect the solar ring, not Umi‑Time itself.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>🔧 Technical / Developer Questions</h2>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">Is Umi‑Time open source?</button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Yes. Umi‑Time is fully open source. The specification, core
            libraries, demos, and website all live in public repositories so
            anyone can inspect, implement, or contribute to the project.
            <br />
            GitHub: <a href="https://github.com/umi-time">github.com/umi-time</a
            >
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >Where can I learn the full specification?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            On the site’s <a href="/spec">Specification</a> page. It includes definitions,
            the computational model, solar‑geometry formulas, textual formats, conformance
            rules, and reference algorithms.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >Is there a quick start guide for developers?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            The <a href="/getting-started">Getting Started</a> page provides a fast
            introduction and a Developer Quickstart example. The
            <a href="/api">API Reference</a> is the main resource for integration.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">Where can I report bugs or issues?</button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            On the project’s GitHub repository:
            <br />
            • <a href="https://github.com/umi-time/umi-time/issues">Issues</a> for
            bugs or suggestions • <a
              href="https://github.com/umi-time/umi-time/discussions"
              >Discussions</a
            > for questions or ideas
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >Are there reference implementations?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Yes. The JavaScript demos show Umi‑Time calculation, dial behavior,
            solar‑day modeling, and map logic:
            <br />
            • <a href="/clock">Clock Demo</a>
            • <a href="/world-map">World Map Demo</a>
            • <a href="/examples">Examples & Demos</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">How stable is the specification?</button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            The core concepts are stable. Wording refinements and edge‑case
            clarifications continue to evolve as the project matures.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">How do I contribute?</button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Join the GitHub Discussions, open issues, or submit pull requests:
            <br />
            • <a href="https://github.com/umi-time/umi-time/discussions"
              >Discussions</a
            >
            • <a href="https://github.com/umi-time/umi-time/issues">Issues</a>
            • <a href="https://github.com/umi-time/umi-time/pulls"
              >Pull Requests</a
            >
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >Is Umi‑Time intended to be standardized formally (IETF, ISO)?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Not yet. The current focus is refining the specification, building
            demos, and growing the community. Formal standardization would only
            make sense once the project has matured and developed a strong
            following.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">What is the long‑term roadmap?</button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            The long‑term roadmap focuses on expanding the Umi‑Time ecosystem.
            Key goals include refining the specification, extending the demos,
            and developing official libraries in multiple languages so Umi‑Time
            can be used across many platforms.
          </p>
          <p>
            Maker‑friendly physical clocks are a near‑term possibility — simple
            designs with a single daily‑rotation hand and a static solar ring
            can be built quickly by hobbyists. More advanced physical
            implementations, such as professional watches, are a longer‑term
            aspiration due to their complexity.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>🖥️ Website‑Specific Questions</h2>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">How do I use the map demo?</button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            <strong>View the full map:</strong> The map scales to your device width.
            Zooming is not supported. Pan or scroll vertically to see the full height.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Set the observer’s location:</strong> Move the mouse pointer or
            tap the map. This updates the solar context and shows the Umi‑Time clock
            for that point on Earth.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What changes:</strong> Sunrise, sunset, twilight progression,
            and dial orientation update with location. The underlying Umi‑Time value
            does not change.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">What does the clock page show?</button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            The clock page displays the live Umi‑Time clock along with local
            solar context:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Umi‑Time dial and primary hand:</strong> Shows the current umi
              value on a 360‑umi dial (one full rotation per UTC day). A secondary
              hand shows the number of seconds elapsed within the current umi unit.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Solar ring:</strong> Always visible, showing sunrise, sunset,
              twilight progression, solar noon, and other solar‑day features for the
              current observer location.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Civil‑time readout:</strong> Your local clock time is shown
              alongside Umi‑Time for easy comparison.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Maximize and restore:</strong> Switch between a compact view
              and a full‑screen style view to focus on the dial and solar ring.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Manual latitude/longitude:</strong> If location services are
              unavailable or you prefer not to grant access, you can manually enter
              coordinates. This sets the solar context while the umi value remains
              the same.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >Why does the Clock demo ask for my location? Is it safe to allow it?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Location is used once to compute local and solar context layers. It
            is not stored, tracked, or sent anywhere.
          </p>
          <p>
            You may also enter latitude and longitude manually via the gear icon
            if you prefer not to grant access.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>❓ Common Misunderstandings</h2>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >Is Umi‑Time just UTC with different units?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            In part, yes. Umi‑Time is derived directly from UTC and represents
            the same moments in time, just expressed with different units.
            However, it adds additional layers of local and solar context that
            UTC alone does not provide.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">Is Umi‑Time just a 24 hour clock?</button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            No. While Umi‑Time represents the same daily cycle as UTC, it is
            built on a layered model. A shared universal umi value can be
            complemented by longitude-based local context and optional solar
            context, providing a richer understanding of the day's progression
            than a conventional clock display.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">Is Umi‑Time just decimal time?</button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            No. Decimal time changes the length of a second; Umi‑Time keeps
            standard seconds and simply maps the 24‑hour UTC day onto 360ᵘ.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >Is Umi‑Time the same as sidereal time?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            No. Sidereal time measures Earth’s rotation relative to the stars.
            Umi‑Time is tied to the UTC day which shifts relative to the stars
            by a small number of degrees each day.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">Does Umi‑Time depend on longitude?</button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            The umi value in Umi‑Time is the same everywhere. Longitude only
            affects the orientation of the local‑context layer, not Umi‑Time
            itself.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">Is Umi‑Time a time zone?</button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            No. Umi‑Time has no offsets, regions, or seasonal rules. It is a
            universal time value, not a civil‑time zone. However, the
            local‑context layer adjusts the representation of Umi‑Time to more
            closely match the observer's day, providing similar functionality to
            a time zone without actually being one.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">Is Umi‑Time a calendar?</button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            No. Umi‑Time represents the time of day. It does not encode dates or
            calendar information, though it may be combined with such
            information from other sources to provide a complete temporal
            context.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">Does Umi‑Time require GPS?</button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            No, but it can make use of location information provided by GPS for
            representing optional local context. Other sources can provide
            location information as well, including Wi‑Fi, system services, or
            manual entry.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >Is Umi‑Time affected by daylight saving time?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            No. DST is a civil‑time rule. Umi‑Time does not use offsets and is
            not affected by daylight‑saving changes.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>🧪 Practical Real‑World Questions</h2>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >Can I use Umi‑Time for scientific work?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Yes — for any workflow based on UTC. Umi‑Time preserves UTC’s
            precision and simply expresses the 24‑hour day as 360ᵘ.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >Can I use Umi‑Time for meditation / mindfulness / circadian tracking?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Yes. The continuous 360ᵘ representation of the solar day provides a
            calm, intuitive sense of daily rhythm that aligns well with
            mindfulness and circadian awareness.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >Does Umi‑Time help with global coordination?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Yes. Everyone shares the same Umi‑Time value with no offsets,
            regions, or seasonal rules, making global coordination
            straightforward.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">Is Umi‑Time useful for astronomy?</button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Yes — especially for solar‑day planning, twilight awareness, and
            observation timing. It complements, but does not replace,
            specialized astronomical time scales such as sidereal time or UT1.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >Is Umi‑Time useful for outdoor photography?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Yes. Umi‑Time makes sunrise, golden hour, blue hour, and sunset easy
            to visualize through the solar ring and daily rotation.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >Is Umi‑Time compatible with existing software systems?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Yes. Umi‑Time is a display‑layer concept built directly on UTC, so
            it integrates cleanly with existing systems and time APIs.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>🧩 Philosophical / Motivational</h2>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question"
          >Why anchor time to the UTC day instead of local time zones?</button
        >
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Time zones are human conventions; UTC is universal. Civil time
            depends on regional rules, offsets, and seasonal adjustments.
            Umi‑Time anchors itself to the UTC day, making it globally
            consistent and independent of borders or legislation.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">Is Umi‑Time political?</button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            No. Umi‑Time does not use time zones, regional offsets, or
            government‑defined rules. It is based entirely on the UTC day — a
            physical process involving atomic clocks that exists outside
            political or cultural boundaries.
